#{[17592186176604 #edb/ref 1061 1242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f16dbb41-7982-5668-b0bb-22d6b038dbe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176673 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176673 #edb/ref 1061 25381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176673 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71c365fe-68f2-5852-89cf-41fb104a5949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176673 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177063 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that we can use the second derivative to determine the concavity of a differentiable function $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) > 0$ on $(a,b),$ while\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) < 0$ on $(a,b).$\n\nSo, to determine intervals of concavity of $f(x)$ by looking at the graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is increasing, then $f''(x)>0,$ which means $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n![](images/8a/8ac780a2dfa4d6c32449b750ff236568764de920d38e363d1badf4c7324c9c94.png)\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is decreasing, then $f''(x)<0,$ which means $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcc5e92916f582dc75450241c0223b55dab287d24e06b55e4d8fde56c7f927c.png)\n\nAlso, recall that $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if $f''(x)$ changes its sign around $x=a.$ So, to determine the inflection points of $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ switches from increasing to decreasing (or vice versa). \n\nIn other words, we need to pay attention to relative extrema on the graph of $f'(x).$\n\n![](images/68/68a0a5523690eceb319bff14916528b6c1727d68e393373553bc42fbf6be6f16.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177063 #edb/ref 1065 649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177063 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8ef5e0fb-85b8-5d0c-890a-85fa61aaef1a" #edb/